          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em photonic crystal
            (
            %a{:href => "#BenistyEtAl99"}> Benisty et a. 1999
            , going back to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Yablonovitch87"} Yablonovitch 1987
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#John87"} John 1987
            = succeed "," do
              %em waveguide array
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#TrompeterEtAl03"} Trompeter et al. 2003
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light+waves"} light waves
            behave like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bloch+waves"} Bloch waves
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            in an actual
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+bands"} energy bands
            separated by band gaps (
            %a{:href => "#Yablonovitch87"}> Yablonovitch 1987
            ). (Of course, the underlying material may itself consist of actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystal"} crystalline
            structures, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BBKKL10"} BBKKL10
          %p
            By suitably engineering photonic crystals they may emulate various phenomena seen elsewhere in nature (for instance transport properties analogous to those in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiconductors"} semiconductors
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metamaterial"} meta-materials
          %p
            In much the same way, there are
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phononic+crystals"} phononic crystals
          %p
            For instance, many aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulators"}> topological insulators
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semimetals"} semimetals
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+photonics"} topological photonics
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesTopologicalPhotonics"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nodal+lines"} nodal lines
            have been demonstrated in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photonic+semimetals"} photonic semimetals
            (
            %a{:href => "#ParkWongZhangOh21"}> Park, Wong, Zhang&amp; Oh 2021
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ParkGaoZhangOh22"} Park, Gao, Zhang &amp; Oh 2022
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phonons"} phonons
            (
            %a{:href => "#PBMS22"}> Peng, Bouhon, Monserrat &amp; Slager 2022
            ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
